= Genomics =

== Overview ==
'''Genomics''' is the field of biology that studies genomes as large-scale systems, including their sequences, genes, regulatory elements, variation, organization, and interactions.

A genome is the complete genetic material of an organism or cell under a specified biological definition. Genomics often analyzes many genes or genome-wide features simultaneously rather than focusing on one locus at a time.

Genomic research includes genome sequencing and assembly, variant analysis, comparative genomics, functional genomics, epigenomics, population genomics, and integration with other molecular data.

Genomics depends heavily on bioinformatics because genome-scale datasets are too large and structured for manual analysis alone. It remains a biological field, not simply a synonym for sequence computation.

== Boundaries ==
* genomics ≠ genetics
* genomics ≠ genome sequencing only
* genome-scale data ≠ biological interpretation by itself

== Related concepts ==
[[Genetics]], [[Bioinformatics]], [[Biological sequence]], [[Sequence annotation]], [[Biological database]], and [[Systems biology]] define the genome-scale context.

== Genome-scale inference ==
Genome assemblies, annotations and variant calls are model-mediated outputs, not exact observations of an organism's complete biology. A draft assembly can contain gaps and errors; an annotated gene model can reflect computational predictions. Comparing assemblies or variant calls requires attention to reference version, sequencing technology, read depth, mapping ambiguity and quality filters.

== Genomic reference frameworks ==
Reference genomes provide coordinates for comparing observations, while a '''pangenome''' represents variation across multiple individuals or lineages. Reference selection influences which variants are readily detectable and comparable. A sequence from one person or isolate is not the exhaustive genetic diversity of a species. Population genomics can estimate patterns of variation, but ancestry and phenotype should not be inferred deterministically from a single marker.

== Computation and biological interpretation ==
[[Related to::Bioinformatics|Bioinformatics]] supplies alignment, assembly, annotation and statistical infrastructure. [[Related to::Biological database|Biological databases]] preserve accessions, versions, sequence annotations and provenance needed for reproducibility. Genome-scale associations may suggest mechanisms but still need appropriate validation; “omics” data quantity is not equivalent to causal explanation.

